    "created_at": "2025-07-29T10:39:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-07-29T10:39:56Z",
    "body": "In pseudo-code example, this would be the difference in implementation:\n\nBefore:\n```js\nWindow w = createWindow();\n\nRealm.setGlobalObject(w); // Window object WITH `window`: `Window{self=...,parent=...,top=...,frames=...,window=...}`\n\n// Expose ECMAScript global properties\nRealm.getGlobalObject().Array = ...; // Expose \"Array\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().Math = ...; // Expose \"Math\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().NaN = ...; // Expose \"Nan\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().Infinity = ...; // Expose \"Infinity\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().parseFloat = ...; // Expose \"parseFloat\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().parseInt = ...; // Expose \"parseInt\"\n```\n\nAfter:\n```js\nWindow w = createWindow();\n\nRealm.setGlobalObject(w); // Window object WITHOUT `window`: `Window{self=...,parent=...,top=...,frames=...}`\n\n// Expose attributes of \"GlobalThisWindow\" (see initial comment)\nRealm.getGlobalObject().window = w; // Expose \"window\"\n\n// Expose ECMAScript global properties\nRealm.getGlobalObject().Array = ...; // Expose \"Array\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().Math = ...; // Expose \"Math\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().NaN = ...; // Expose \"Nan\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().Infinity = ...; // Expose \"Infinity\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().parseFloat = ...; // Expose \"parseFloat\"\nRealm.getGlobalObject().parseInt = ...; // Expose \"parseInt\"\n```\n\nI think its an improvement in a way that it clearifies why there is a `window` in the first place. If I would use a global object without any properties instead (`Realm.setGlobalObject(emptyObjectWithoutProperties);`), I would have a problem in the \"before\" example, because there is no way to get the Window object and the `window` property is part of that object. In the \"after\" example, I could just use `window`, because the `window` property gets exposed. The fact that the Window object is also used as global object is purely convenience.\n\n---\n\n- I think that `self` is on the Window object just because `top` and `parent` are there too: completeness.\n- Technically, the `frames` could have been an `Array`. It may have been better if it was, but @BrendanEich choose to reuse the Window object and make it a pseudo-array.",
